Kent Paul Dolan <xanthian@well.com> wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
>
> I may be wrong on that guess, though. What was once
> "Usenet" with a few dozen newsgroups is now
> "Usenet-like" with, last time I saw, 120,000
> "newsgroups". That splits the audience pretty
> dramatically. In all that clutter, even finding any
> one newsgroup is challenging.

Although a Google web search does also show hits from Google Groups.

So with the right keywords even an uninformed user might stumble upon
this group.

> Still, with over 100,000 downloads per year, and the
> game around for 20 years, I don't feel too bad about
> guessing that two years worth of NetHack downloaders
> are also rgrn readers.
>
>> But besides the IRC channel I don't know a place
>> with a NetHack community big enough.
>
> Wouldn't you expect almost all of them to _also_ be
> reading here?

From time to time? Yes.

Regularly? No. 

There are of course regularly links from #nethack to rgrn, but why
should almost all #nethack users read rgrn?

For spoilers nethack.wikia.com is better. For instant help, #nethack
is better.

rgrn is of course better in regards of deeper discussions. It is not
easy to get a good discussion if you write only few lines and don't
have threads to keep different discussions separate.

> IRC is a dreadful communications mechanism, IMO.

It is different from Usenet. It is more of a synchronous messaging
system in contrast to Usenets asynchronous nature. And in a crowded
channel it is hard to keep track of each other.

>> I guess that there are players who would oppose a
>> NetHack successor that would not be a fork from
>> the current code base.
>
> Why on earth? _Anything_ that worked as NetHack
> works, but was in some form of maintainable code,
> would be a huge win for the whole community, both in
> terms of likelihood of being low on bugs, and in
> terms of allowing exploration of game additions by
> user-written patches.

*If* it works like NetHack and *if* it has fewer bugs and *if*
modding/scripting is easier than patching is now.

I'm rather surprised why the criticism in the other thread about a
rewrite of NetHack shouldn't apply here, too.

Having a finished and working roguelike engine as a foundation of a
rewrite is of course much better than starting from scratch. But
there's still the whole behavior of NetHack that would have to be
implemented with it. This is no small feat to accomplish.


If I were Ray I'd ask myself what would be more fun to me or what
would be more satisfying to me:
- creating a clone of NetHack with none/few/lots of improvements over
  vanilla
- or creating a completely new roguelike with new content but with
  probably a lot less complexity than NetHack.


One reason, although not a rational one, why NeoHack could be
shunned by some players is exactly the one that JG Miller is always
propagating.

It wouldn't be NetHack or a NetHack variant/fork as it would break the
code lineage from the first version of Hack. NeoHack would be a clone
or a rewrite of NetHack in the same way that Hack was "only" a clone
and not a variant of Rogue.


As I already said, I wouldn't mind if NeoHack doesn't build upon the
code base of vanilla NetHack and if it's good I could imagine that it
would find its audience and even if it's really good that when the
DevTeam finally releases its new version the reaction of all but a few
players could be "nice, but we got something better now".

